{
  "$comment": "Hand-edited. One entry per service: the name a person is shown, every spelling a box reports it under, and the string each platform needs to launch it. Add a service or fill in a platform id and open a PR — controllers pick it up within a day, with no release. `name` and `slug` are matched as aliases automatically; `launch` is sparse, and an absent platform means nobody has filled that one in yet, not that the app is unavailable there. `icon` is a link to the brand's own published artwork, never a copy of it — this repository redistributes no logos — and is only consulted for a platform that cannot list what it has installed, since a box that lists its own apps reports its own artwork with them. Today that means SmartCast, so most of them come from `mobileAppInfo.app_icon_image_url` in scfs.vizio.com/appservice/vizio_apps_prod.json.",
  "platforms": {
    "roku": "ECP channel id — `POST /launch/<id>`. Rarely needed: a Roku lists its own channels.",
    "apple_tv": "Bundle id for `_launchApp`. A paired Apple TV reports the bundles it has; this covers the ones it does not.",
    "android_tv": "Android package. Google TV, Shield, Sony, and most Android-based sets.",
    "fire_tv": "Android package as Amazon ships it — often different from android_tv for the same service.",
    "webos": "LG app id. LG re-issued several of these in 2024; where a set disagrees, its own list wins.",
    "tizen": "What Samsung's remote API takes, which is a name rather than a numeric app id.",
    "vizio": "VIZIO's own `app_type_payload`, copied verbatim — the body of `PUT /app/launch`. Take it from scfs.vizio.com/appservice/app_availability_prod.json, joined to vizio_apps_prod.json by `id`. Kept whole rather than split into NAME_SPACE/APP_ID because MESSAGE is load-bearing for some apps (Pluto carries a nested Cast payload in it)."
  },
